# 121 keeps two of `builtin_terminals[]`' ten rows -- `xterm-256color` and `debug` --
# and is THE FIRST PART II PHASE TO DECLARE `term-moved`.  The token has been in this
# grammar since phase 83 and no phase has used it: the terminal table was nineteen
# ways of recording that the environment decided nothing until phase 116 changed
# the question to `+set term={name}`, and this is the phase that question was changed
# for.  So the declaration is a real delta and not a harness artefact -- which is the
# distinction GOALS.md core rule 2 and CLAUDE.md's *A phase can break a harness rather
# than change behaviour* exist to keep apart, and this phase is on the other side of
# it from phase 88's.
#
# `term-moved` is a whole-file token, so what MOVED is written here rather than left
# to it.  EIGHT of the nineteen rows move and every one of them is a name this phase
# deletes:
#
#   xterm  screen  screen-256color  tmux  tmux-256color  vt100  ansi  dumb
#
# each from `term=<itself>` -- the only answer that says a name reached a table -- to
# `E522 term=xterm-256color t_Co=256`, which is the answer the eight names that never
# had a row (`alacritty-256color vt320 vt52 iris-ansi pcansi win32 amiga
# no-such-term-9x`) already gave.  A refused name leaves the terminal alone, and what
# it is left at is MEASURED from the binary rather than written down.
#
# THE OTHER ELEVEN ROWS ARE BYTE-IDENTICAL.  `xterm-256color` and `debug` still
# resolve to themselves; the eight above were already refusals; and `''` is still
# E529, which is `'term'` refusing an empty string before any table is consulted.
#
# NOTHING ELSE MOVES, and the two things that could have are worth naming.  The 102
# screen cases and the 98 Ex-command rows are recorded on a pipe where the
# window-size ioctl fails and the compiled default applies, and that default is
# `xterm-256color`, which keeps its row.  And `-T {term}` reaches `set_termname()`
# BEFORE there is a screen, where an unknown name is not refused but replaced by a
# fallback written in the source -- and that fallback named `xterm`, which this phase
# deletes.  Left alone it would have moved `-T xterm` and `-T no-such-term-9x` into
# `E437: Terminal capability "cm" required`, an editor with no cursor motion, drawing
# 2,045 bytes where the baseline draws 2,117.  The phase retargets the fallback onto
# the compiled default instead, so those two rows are the baseline's again and the
# declaration below is the whole of the difference.  phase/121/check.sh builds the
# repair-left-out form as a control and requires it to move exactly those two.
#
# NOT DECLARED BECAUSE NO HARNESS ASKS IT, and stated here rather than omitted:
# THE `xterm` ROW WAS THE WHOLE XTERM FAMILY AND NOT ONE NAME.  `find_builtin_term()`
# carried a special case -- if the ROW is called `xterm` and `vim_is_xterm(term)`,
# that row's table serves -- so every name `vim_is_xterm()` accepts resolved through
# it: anything beginning with `xterm`, `nxterm`, `kterm`, `mlterm`, `rxvt` or
# `screen.xterm`, and `builtin_xterm` exactly, with `xterm-kitty` the one exclusion.
# Deleting the row deletes all of it, and only the exact `xterm-256color` is left.
# None of those names is among the nineteen `tools/termcheck.py` asks about, so the
# phase owes probes and phase/121/check.sh runs six, derived from
# `vim_is_xterm()`'s own prefix list: every one resolves on the binary the phase was
# handed and every one is E522 here.  `:set term=builtin_xterm` is the same finding
# through `term_is_builtin()`, which strips the prefix and leaves `xterm`.
#
# And the clause itself goes with the row, in the EDIT and not the sweep: once no row
# is called `xterm` its first conjunct is false for every row, gcc has no warning for
# a condition that is false at run time, and nothing in tools/sweep.sh reads one.
term-moved
